INT. CAR IMPOUND LOT - OFFICE - NIGHT

A prefab shed with a buzzing light and a bulletproof window. Wall of keys on numbered hooks. A space heater ticks. Beyond the glass, rows of towed cars shine like sleeping animals under floodlights.

IMELDA ROQUE (50s, night clerk, cardigan, reading glasses, a paperback face-down on the desk) sits at a counter, eating crackers one at a time. DOBBS (30s, tow driver, grease on his sleeves) sprawls in the visitor's chair, sipping a soda, tapping his boot. The radio on the desk crackles with a disembodied voice.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
(over radio)
Unit twelve, pickup at Fifth and Mercer, black sedan, plates blocked. Over.

DOBBS
(into radio)
Copy.

IMELDA
Dobbs, you're on break.

DOBBS
Dispatch doesn't know that.

The glass door chimes. A MAN (40s, wet trench coat, hat low, hands deep in his pockets) steps in, shaking rain off his shoulders. He takes in the room, the counter, the wall of keys, the clerk, the driver. His eyes settle on the keys.

MAN
Evening. I'm here for a car.

IMELDA
License and registration.

MAN
It's not mine. It's a friend's.

IMELDA
Then your friend can come get it.

MAN
My friend's indisposed.

Dobbs puts down his soda. The Man lifts one hand from his pocket, slowly, and lays a thick envelope on the counter. Imelda doesn't touch it. The Man produces a driver's license and slides it across. She picks it up, tilts it toward the lamp, reads.

IMELDA
Mr. Rafferty. Francis Rafferty.

RAFFERTY
Frank.

IMELDA
Mr. Rafferty. This says Ohio.

RAFFERTY
Ohio is where I'm from.

IMELDA
The car is Illinois.

RAFFERTY
It's a very long drive.

Dobbs stands, stretching, and drifts toward the door. Imelda watches him go. She hands the license back and takes the envelope in the same motion, slides it into a drawer, closes the drawer with her knee. Rafferty smiles faintly.

IMELDA
Space nine. Black Chrysler. I'll need you to sign.

RAFFERTY
Of course.

She slides a form across. Rafferty bends to sign. Dobbs, by the door, puts a hand on the knob and stops. Through the glass, the floodlights flicker once, and all down the long rows of impounded cars, a headlight comes on.

Then another. Then ten.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
(over radio)
Unit twelve, disregard the pickup. All units, return to the lot immediately. I repeat, return to the lot.

Dobbs snatches the radio. Imelda lifts her glasses onto her forehead. Rafferty finishes his signature, caps the pen, and sets it down on the form with great care.

DOBBS
(into radio)
Dispatch, what's going on?

DISPATCH (V.O.)
(over radio)
Dobbs, don't let the man leave.

RAFFERTY
Mr. Dobbs, I'd like to leave now.

IMELDA
You'd better sit.

RAFFERTY
I'm afraid I can't.

Outside, one by one, the engines catch. A low thunder rises across the lot. Rafferty looks over his shoulder at the glass, then at Imelda, then at the keys on the wall that are, one by one, beginning to swing.

RAFFERTY (CONT'D)
They always do this when I come for one.

He tips his hat. The door chimes open on its own. Rafferty walks out into the glare of a hundred headlights, and the engines fall silent as he passes.
